# The only env example in this repo. Copy to `.env.local` at the repo ROOT and
# fill in what you need; `.env.local` is gitignored.
#
# One file, at the root, for everything: `npm run dev` reads it (through
# frontend/vite-dev-api.ts) and so do the python tools. There used to be one
# per directory, which meant the same token pasted twice and drifting.
#
# Everything here is optional - modSUTD runs as a fully static site without any
# of it. A var with no value switches its path off; nothing fails loudly.
#
# NEVER prefix any of these VITE_. Vite inlines a VITE_ var verbatim into the
# public bundle, which publishes it to every visitor.
#
# Where each of these goes - Actions, Vercel, this file, or more than one - is
# the table in docs/architecture.md, along with the two other things that are
# easy to get wrong. One copy, so two cannot drift.

# ---- LLM providers (optional) -----------------------------------------

# Tried in this order, first valid answer wins: Gemini, then Groq, then OpenAI.
# Configuring one is enough - a provider with no token is skipped, not failed.
# With none set, PR review stays dormant and the scraper is deterministic-only.
# Read by tools/scraper/agents/llm.py, which is what every model call in this
# repo goes through, and by .github/workflows/code-review.yml and
# .github/workflows/scrape.yml as repository secrets of the same names.
# Locally, agents/llm.py reads them out of .env.local at the repo root; a real
# environment variable always wins, so CI reads nothing off disk.
#
# Without one, `mods_refresh.py`'s `propose` step says so in its report and
# proposes nothing. The drift report is still written and the PR still opens.
#
# A token is the only thing to set. The endpoint and the model live in code;
# <PROVIDER>_MODEL overrides the model without a code change, which is what you
# want the day one of those ids is retired.

GEMINI_TOKEN=
GROQ_TOKEN=
OPENAI_TOKEN=

# Defaults in code: gemini-3.8-flash, openai/gpt-oss-20b, gpt-5.4-mini.
# GEMINI_MODEL=
# GROQ_MODEL=
# OPENAI_MODEL=

# ---- GitHub ------------------------------------------------------------

# Fine-grained PAT on modsutd-community/modsutd. Contents: read and write,
# Pull requests: read and write, Discussions: read and write.
# Powers the scrape PR in Actions, and /api/contribute, /api/review-thread and
# /api/telegram-group in the browser. Without it the scraper falls back to
# GITHUB_TOKEN and all three relays answer 503.
MODSUTD_BOT_TOKEN=

# Public client id of the modSUTD OAuth App - an OAuth App, NOT a GitHub App:
# /api/gh-device-code asks for `scope: 'gist public_repo'`, and a GitHub App
# ignores scopes entirely. Settings -> Developer settings -> OAuth Apps -> New
# OAuth App, then tick "Enable Device Flow" on the app page. Without it
# "link github" 503s and records stay local to the browser. No client SECRET is
# needed: the device flow is a public client.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=

# ---- Telegram batch-chat tooling (optional) ----------------------------

# From my.telegram.org. tools/telegram runs on a StringSession that only ever
# lives in GitHub Actions, so nothing here writes a session file.
TG_API_ID=
TG_API_HASH=

# Printed by `python tools/telegram/login.py` after an SMS re-auth. This is full
# access to the throwaway Telegram account - it belongs in GitHub Actions
# secrets only. Never Vercel, never this repo, never copied between machines.
TG_SESSION=

# 32 random bytes, base64: `openssl rand -base64 32`. Encrypts the invite links
# on the write side. The identical value has to reach Vercel as TG_LINK_KEY for
# the read side, or /api/telegram-link decrypts to garbage rather than erroring.
TG_LINK_KEY=

# Optional. Username only, never a token - the seed bot that is added at group
# creation and leaves immediately afterwards.
TG_BOT_USERNAME=

# Optional, and only for login.py on a network that blocks raw MTProto.
# TG_PROXY=socks5://host:port
# TG_MTPROXY=host:port:secret

# ---- freshness check (optional) ----------------------------------------

# Origin of the deployed site. When set, tools/scraper/freshness.py fetches
# <SITE_URL>/data/manifest.json and reports drift if the live counts disagree
# with the repo, which is how a merge that never shipped gets noticed.
SITE_URL=

# ---- deploy (Actions only) ---------------------------------------------

# Project Settings -> Git -> Deploy Hooks, pointed at main. deploy.yml POSTs
# it and Vercel builds server-side, so nothing here repeats the build settings.
# No token: the URL carries its own secret, which is also why it belongs in
# Actions secrets and nowhere else.
# VERCEL_DEPLOY_HOOK=
